## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of three key concepts: Trie mutations (insert/delete), weighted dynamic programming for word break problems, and the ability to implement both iterative and recursive approaches. It also evaluates understanding of handling dynamic dictionary updates and edge cases such as when no valid decomposition exists.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal weights for efficient prefix matching
- Dynamic insert/delete operations on Trie structure
- Weighted word-break DP with O(n × L) complexity where L = maxWordLen
- Both iterative bottom-up DP and memoized top-down DFS variants

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.endWeight = 0;
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}

class WeightedTrie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.maxWordLen = 0;
  }

  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
    node.endWeight = weight;
    this.maxWordLen = Math.max(this.maxWordLen, word.length);
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return;
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.isEnd = false;
    node.endWeight = 0;
  }

  maxPartition(s) {
    const n = s.length;
    const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
    dp[n] = 0;

    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      let node = this.root;
      for (let j = i; j < Math.min(n, i + this.maxWordLen); j++) {
        const char = s[j];
        if (!node.children.has(char)) break;
        node = node.children.get(char);
        if (node.isEnd && dp[j + 1] !== -1) {
          dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], node.endWeight + dp[j + 1]);
        }
      }
    }
    return dp[0];
  }

  maxPartitionDFS(s, memo = {}) {
    if (s.length === 0) return 0;
    if (s in memo) return memo[s];

    let maxWeight = -1;
    let node = this.root;

    for (let i = 0; i < Math.min(s.length, this.maxWordLen); i++) {
      const char = s[i];
      if (!node.children.has(char)) break;
      node = node.children.get(char);

      if (node.isEnd) {
        const remaining = this.maxPartitionDFS(s.slice(i + 1), memo);
        if (remaining !== -1) {
          maxWeight = Math.max(maxWeight, node.endWeight + remaining);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[s] = maxWeight;
    return maxWeight;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of prefix-based searching using a practical, lightweight data structure. It combines Trie fundamentals with per-node augmentation to enable fast prefix queries in real time—useful in autocomplete systems, IP routing, and spell checking.

## Key Concepts
- **Trie structure**: Each node contains 26 children (one per letter) and maintains a pass counter representing how many words pass through that node
- **insert operation**: O(m) time, where m is word length; creates nodes as needed and increments pass at each node visited
- **count_with_prefix operation**: O(k) time, where k is prefix length; traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass counter
- **Handling duplicates**: Pass counters naturally accumulate, so duplicate insertions simply increment the counter without special handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.pass = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) node.next[idx] = new TrieNode();
      node.pass++;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    node.pass++;
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) return 0;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
}

// Example usage
const trie = new Trie();
['cat', 'car', 'cart', 'dog', 'dot'].forEach(w => trie.insert(w));
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('ca')); // 3
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('car')); // 1
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('d')); // 2
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('do')); // 2
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph traversal algorithms, BFS implementation, and pathfinding skills - critical for autonomous vehicle navigation systems and route planning applications.

## Expected Answer
The candidate should implement BFS using a queue, maintain a visited set to avoid cycles, track distances from the start node, and handle edge cases such as when start equals target, disconnected graphs, or empty input. They should also explain why BFS guarantees the shortest path in unweighted graphs.

## Code Example
```python
from collections import deque

def shortest_path(graph, start, end):
    if start == end:
        return 0

    queue = deque([(start, 0)])
    visited = {start}

    while queue:
        node, distance = queue.popleft()

        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):
            if neighbor == end:
                return distance + 1
            if neighbor not in visited:
                visited.add(neighbor)
                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))

    return -1
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests practical DP skills: counting subsets, managing zeros, and comparing memoization vs tabulation.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting
- Top-down memoization
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and zero handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsetsTopDown(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Map());
  function dfs(i, t){
    if (t === 0) return 1;
    if (i === n) return 0;
    const key = t;
    if (memo[i].has(key)) return memo[i].get(key);
    let res = dfs(i+1, t);
    if (t >= nums[i]) res = (res + dfs(i+1, t - nums[i]));
    memo[i].set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}
function countSubsetsBottomUp(nums, target){
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const v of nums){
    for (let w = target; w >= v; w--){
      dp[w] = (dp[w] + dp[w - v]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
``` 

